{{- $c := .Local -}}
{{- $cli := $c.CliName -}}
This CLI is built to be driven by AI coding agents as well as people: everything an agent needs is discoverable from the binary itself, and every command can be validated without credentials. Work down this ladder:

| Run | You get |
|-----|---------|
| `{{$cli}} --help`, `{{$cli}} {{$c.ShowcasePath}} --help` | Commands by category, runnable examples, flags |
| `{{$cli}} --usage`, `{{$cli}} {{$c.ShowcasePath}} --usage` | The command surface as machine-readable [KDL](https://kdl.dev): commands, aliases, flags, defaults, env vars, config keys |
{{- if $c.Schema}}
| `{{$c.Schema}} --schema` | The exact JSON Schema of the command's request body (all `$ref`s bundled) — build a valid `--body` from it |
{{- end}}
| `{{$c.Showcase}} --dry-run` | The exact HTTP request (method, URL, headers, body), with no credentials or network call |
| `{{$c.Showcase}} --output-format json` (or `--jq`) | Machine-readable output |

### Discover the command surface

```bash
# Every command, flag, default, env var and config key, as KDL
{{$cli}} --usage

# One command's subtree only
{{$cli}} {{$c.ShowcasePath}} --usage
```
{{- if $c.Schema}}

### Read the exact request schema

`--schema` is available on every command that accepts a request body (`--body`, stdin, or a whole-body flag where the command has one), including intent commands. It prints the JSON Schema the request is validated against and exits without calling the API.

```bash
# JSON Schema (draft 2020-12) of the request body, with every $ref bundled under $defs
{{$c.Schema}} --schema
```
{{- end}}

### Probe before you spend

Start quota-spending commands with `--dry-run`. It validates inputs, resolves the request, redacts secrets and binary payloads, makes no network call, and exits 0. It never reads the OS keychain; credentials supplied by flag, environment, or config file are included only as `[REDACTED]`.

```bash
# Human preview: the [DRY-RUN] block is on stderr and stdout is empty
{{$c.Showcase}} --dry-run
{{- if $c.Intent}}
{{$c.Intent}} --dry-run
{{- end}}

# Machine preview: compact JSON on stdout and silent stderr
{{$c.Showcase}} --dry-run --output-format json
```

The machine form writes one object per would-be request, one per line (NDJSON for multi-request commands), with exactly this shape:

```json
{"dry_run":true,"request":{"method":"POST","url":"https://…","headers":{"Accept":["application/json"],…},"body":<JSON value | string | null>}}
```

`body` is a parsed JSON value when the body is JSON, a string for text, `"<bytes:N>"` for binary data, and `null` when absent. An explicit caller `--jq` also selects this JSON preview protocol, but the filter is not applied to preview objects. Command-declared jq presets do not select or filter the preview.

Local mutation commands make no request under `--dry-run`: instead of a preview they emit one `{"dry_run":true,"local":true,"command":"…","message":"…"}` object. `select(.request)` keeps only would-be requests; `select(.local)` keeps the local no-ops.

### Machine-readable output

```bash
# JSON on stdout
{{$c.Showcase}} --output-format json

# Filter or reshape with a jq expression (always emits JSON, overrides --output-format)
{{$c.Showcase}} --jq '.'

# Print jq string results as plain text instead of JSON strings (like jq -r)
{{$c.Showcase}} --jq '.' --raw-output
```

`--output-format toon` emits [TOON](https://github.com/toon-format/spec), a compact line-oriented format that uses fewer tokens than JSON; it is the default in agent mode.

{{- if isAnyInteractiveEnabled}}

### Interactive mode

{{- if interactiveByDefault}}
{{- if and (isInteractiveModeEnabled) (isInteractiveAuthEnabled)}}
Required-input prompts and guided `configure` / `auth login` forms are enabled by default. Required-input prompts require an interactive terminal; off-TTY forms read line input from stdin. Use `--no-interactive` to force flag-only execution.
{{- else if isInteractiveModeEnabled}}
Required-input prompts are enabled by default when stdin and stdout are interactive terminals. Use `--no-interactive` to disable prompting.
{{- else}}
Guided `configure` / `auth login` forms are enabled by default; off-TTY forms read line input from stdin. Use `--no-interactive` to force flag-only execution.
{{- end}}
{{- else}}
{{- if and (isInteractiveModeEnabled) (isInteractiveAuthEnabled)}}
This CLI is non-interactive by default. Pass `--interactive` to prompt for missing inputs or open guided `configure` / `auth login` forms. Required-input prompts require an interactive terminal; off-TTY forms read line input from stdin.
{{- else if isInteractiveModeEnabled}}
This CLI is non-interactive by default. Pass `--interactive` to prompt for missing inputs when stdin and stdout are interactive terminals.
{{- else}}
This CLI is non-interactive by default. Pass `--interactive` to open guided `configure` / `auth login` forms; off-TTY forms read line input from stdin.
{{- end}}
{{- end}}

```bash
{{- if isInteractiveModeEnabled}}
# Prompt for missing command inputs
{{- if $c.HasIntents}}
{{$cli}} {{$c.IntentName}} --interactive
{{- else}}
{{$cli}} {{$c.ShowcasePath}} --interactive
{{- end}}
{{- end}}
{{- if isInteractiveAuthEnabled}}

# Open the guided configuration form
{{$cli}} configure --interactive
{{- end}}
{{- if isInteractiveModeEnabled}}

# Explicitly launch the terminal command explorer
{{$cli}} explore
{{- end}}
```
{{- end}}

### Agent mode and structured errors

{{- if agentEnvironmentDetection}}
Agent mode turns on automatically when a known agent environment is detected (`CLAUDECODE`, `CURSOR_AGENT`, `CODEX`, `AIDER`, `CLINE`, `WINDSURF_AGENT`, `GITHUB_COPILOT`, `AMAZON_Q`, `GEMINI_CODE_ASSIST`, `SRC_CODY`) or with `--agent-mode` (`--agent-mode=false` disables detection).
{{- else}}
Agent mode turns on only when explicitly requested with `--agent-mode`; environment variables do not identify the caller.
{{- end}}
In agent mode interactive prompts never launch, output defaults to TOON, and every failure — API errors and CLI usage errors alike — is one JSON envelope on stderr:

{{- if classifiedErrors}}
`--output-format json` and `--jq` use the same error envelope without requiring agent mode.
{{- else}}
Outside agent mode, explicit JSON and `--jq` preserve the compatibility envelope without classification; enable agent mode to request the classified contract.
{{- end}}

```json
{
  "error": "...",
  "error_type": "validation_error",
  "error_reason": "CLI_VALIDATION",
  "exit_code": 2,
  "message": "human-readable message",
  "hints": ["what to try next"]
}
```

{{- $carriers := cliErrorCarrierDisplayList}}

`error_type` is one of `authentication_error`, `authorization_error`, `not_found`, `validation_error`, `rate_limit_error`, `server_error`, `api_error`, `connection_error`, `protocol_error`, {{if cliEmitsServiceDisabledErrorType}}`service_disabled`, {{end}}{{if cliEmitsBillingDisabledErrorType}}`billing_disabled`, {{end}}`runtime_error`, `unsupported_error`, `async_failed`, `async_timeout`, `async_unknown_state`. {{if $carriers}}Classification reads the structured reason code at {{$carriers}} in the error body (resolved against the nested `error` object when the body has one) before HTTP status, so a declared credential reason sent with HTTP 400 is not mistaken for request validation. {{if cliErrorAnyCarrierDeclared}}`error_reason` carries the reason code found there, verbatim from a declared carrier when no declared rule matches it{{else}}`error_reason` carries the reason code found there when a declared rule matches it (an undeclared body reason is never promoted){{end}}; it is absent for status-only API errors.{{else}}Classification derives from the HTTP status and transport evidence; `error_reason` is absent for API errors.{{end}} Status-less local failures may use `CLI_VALIDATION`, `CLI_CONNECTION`, `CLI_PROTOCOL`, `CLI_RUNTIME`, `CLI_UNAVAILABLE`, `CLI_AUTHENTICATION`, the async polling reasons `CLI_ASYNC_FAILED`, `CLI_ASYNC_TIMEOUT`, and `CLI_ASYNC_UNKNOWN_STATE`, or `CLI_OUTCOME_FAILED` when a successful response reports a declared failure state; that envelope also carries `state` (the reported state) and, when the command declares an error pointer, `error` (the failure detail taken from the response). `hints` preserves server guidance first, adds the most specific local taxonomy guidance, then typed CLI and command-specific guidance, removing exact duplicates. `exit_code` is always the code for the final `error_type` shown in the envelope: 1 runtime, 2 usage, or 3 authentication/authorization.
{{- if $c.Hints}}

```bash
# "{{$c.HintsIntentName}}" declares hints for {{$c.HintsReasons}}; a failing request returns them in the envelope
{{$c.Hints}}
```
{{- end}}
{{- if or $c.Paginated $c.Streaming $c.Artifact $c.Async}}

### Lists, streams, and files
{{- if $c.Paginated}}

List commands accept `--all` to fetch every page and stream results as they arrive (one JSON value per line with `--output-format json`; `--max-pages N` bounds the walk).

Structured output and agent mode never write pagination hints to stderr; if a later page fails or the server repeats a cursor, the command exits non-zero after the pages already written.

```bash
{{$c.Paginated}} --all --output-format json
```
{{- end}}
{{- if $c.Streaming}}

Streaming commands write each event as it arrives (one JSON object per line with `--output-format json`; a declared streamed projection prints just the selected text{{if $c.StreamSelectPointer}}, e.g. `{{$c.StreamSelectPointer}}`{{end}}):

```bash
{{$c.Streaming}} --output-format json
```
{{- end}}
{{- if $c.Artifact}}

Commands that produce media write the file and print only its path on stdout (`--out <path-or-dir>` chooses the location{{if $c.ArtifactDefaultPath}}, default `./{{$c.ArtifactDefaultPath}}`{{end}}; `--raw-response` prints the API response instead):

```bash
{{$c.Artifact}} --out ./output/
```
{{- end}}
{{- if $c.Async}}

Long-running commands poll to a terminal response; human progress goes to stderr and machine-mode success keeps stderr silent. Add `--async` to `{{$c.Async}}` to return its handle immediately, or tune foreground polling with `--poll-interval <duration>` and `--poll-timeout <duration>`. Resume an escaped or timed-out operation with `{{$c.AsyncResume}} <id>`.
{{- end}}
{{- end}}
